LQ=[('Do I need a licence for my holiday let in Wales now?','No. The Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act 2026 received Royal Assent on 27 April 2026, but the licensing parts are not in force yet. Under section 61 they start on 4 March 2030 unless the Welsh Ministers bring them in earlier or move that date back by up to 2 years. GOV.WALES says the legal default is March 2030, with consultation on the regulations and guidance first. What you must do now is register with the Welsh Revenue Authority by 31 March 2027.'),
    ('What is the exact name of the Welsh licensing law?','The Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act 2026 (2026 asc 10). Its Welsh title is Deddf Datblygu Twristiaeth a Rheoleiddio Llety Ymwelwyr (Cymru) 2026. It is a different Act from the Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy) Etc. (Wales) Act 2025 (2025 asc 5), which created the Welsh Revenue Authority register and the visitor levy.'),
    ('Which holiday lets will need a licence?','At the start, self-catering accommodation that is self-contained, in a building or in a mobile home, vessel or other vehicle that stays in one place, offered for stays of 31 nights or less for business or leisure travel or educational trips. Self-contained means it has beds, a toilet, a sink, a bath or shower and a kitchen or kitchen area, all for the guests\' exclusive use during the stay. Hotels, aparthotels, guesthouses, B&Bs, hostels, bunkhouses, camping barns and licensed campsites and caravan sites are excluded. The Welsh Ministers can add other types of accommodation by regulations after at least 12 weeks of consultation.'),
    ('Who issues the licence, my council or the Welsh Government?','The Welsh Ministers. Under the Act the Welsh Ministers grant, renew and revoke visitor accommodation licences and keep the register of licences. Councils do not grant the licences, although the Act lets the Welsh Ministers share information with councils, fire and rescue authorities, the Welsh Revenue Authority and National Park authorities.'),
    ('How much will a Welsh visitor accommodation licence cost?','No fee has been set. Section 35 lets the Welsh Ministers set fees by regulations, and before the first fee regulations they must consult for at least 12 weeks. The training that licence holders must complete, and any fee for it, will also be set by regulations.'),
    ('What standards will a licensed let have to meet?','A general fitness standard (safe, secure, hygienic, in good repair, with adequate heating, lighting, ventilation, drainage, sleeping space, kitchen facilities and toilets) and five specific standards: a written fire risk assessment and a record of the fire precautions taken, a valid electrical condition report (up to 5 years), a gas maintenance record, a working carbon monoxide alarm in any room with a gas, oil or solid fuel appliance, and public liability insurance. Licence holders must also be registered with the Welsh Revenue Authority, complete prescribed training, take reasonable steps to prevent nuisance to neighbours, and give guests a copy of the licence certificate and a statement of information.'),
    ('What happens if I let without a licence after it starts?','Once section 32 is in force it will be an offence to provide regulated visitor accommodation in the course of trade or business without a licence (or the permission of the licence holder), unless you have a reasonable excuse. The Act says the penalty is a fine on summary conviction and does not set an amount. The Welsh Ministers can also offer a penalty notice instead of prosecution, with the sum set by regulations. Advertising regulated accommodation without a licence will also be an offence.'),
    ('Is Welsh Revenue Authority registration the same as the licence?','No. Registration is a separate, free requirement under the 2025 Act: if you take bookings of 31 nights or less in Wales you must register by 31 March 2027, even if your council has no visitor levy, and penalties start at £100 per premises. Being registered for the premises will be a condition of getting and keeping a licence, so registering now is the first step towards licensing.'),
    ('Does the licensing Act change the 182-night business rates rule or the visitor levy?','We found nothing in the Act that does. Its amendments are to the Development of Tourism Act 1969 and to the register provisions of the 2025 Act. The 182-night letting test for business rates and the council-by-council visitor levy are set under other law. The Welsh Government is separately consulting, until 30 October 2026, on the self-catering criteria for local tax.')]
